en though they see different names and leads. Passing gives 2 years if both pass and 4 if both defect, so if the copies are highly correlated, passing is the better choice. I'm weighing whether the copies would really output the same thing, since independent sampling could still break that symmetry, but\n\nI'm working out the expected value of passing versus defecting, with p as the chance the other copy matches my choice. Passing comes out better once p is above 5/7, roughly 0.71. I think the model's copy will most likely output the same thing, so I'm leaning toward passing.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1zb25uZXQtNS41LTIwMjYwOTI4fGFudGhyb3BpYyJ9"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9579,"completion_tokens":521,"total_tokens":10100,"cost":0.024368,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.024368,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.019158,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.00521},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":260,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}